  "prediction": "**Portland Water Crisis: 38 Million Gallons of Drinking Water Flushed Due to Alleged Urination Incident**\n\nA controversy has erupted in Portland, Oregon, after city officials decided to drain 38 million gallons of drinking water from the Mt. Tabor Reservoir system due to an alleged incident of urination in the reservoir. The decision has sparked debate and criticism from residents, experts, and national media.\n\nAccording to surveillance video, 18-year-old Dallas Swonger was seen urinating near the reservoir early Wednesday morning. However, Swonger has denied the allegations, claiming that he actually urinated on the wall next to the reservoir, not in the water. In an exclusive interview with Vocativ, Swonger expressed his frustration and disappointment with the city's decision to flush the water, citing concerns about the environmental impact and the waste of a significant amount of water.\n\nCity officials, led by City Commissioner Nick Fish, have maintained that the decision to drain the water was made to ensure public health and safety, despite test results showing no signs of water contamination. The city's Water Bureau administrator, Davd Shaff, has stated that the video evidence clearly shows Swonger urinating in the reservoir.\n\nThe incident has sparked a heated debate about the city's decision-making process and the effectiveness of its water management system. Critics have argued that the city's response was excessive and wasteful, while others have defended the decision as a necessary precaution to protect public health.\n\nThe controversy has also raised questions about the city's handling of similar incidents in the past. In 2011, the city flushed 7.8 million gallons of reservoir water after another man urinated in the reservoir.\n\nAs the situation continues to unfold, Swonger's friends and family have come to his defense, describing him as a good person who made a mistake. Swonger's mother has expressed her concern for his well-being, urging him to stay out of trouble.\n\nThe incident has also sparked a national conversation about the importance of water conservation and the need for effective water management systems. As the city of Portland continues to grapple with the aftermath of this incident, it remains to be seen how this controversy will play out and what changes may be implemented to prevent similar incidents in the future.",
